{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nAll four `AUTODEPLOY_REVIEW_INTERRUPT` / `bound-exceeded` firings on 2026-08-12 missed the\n`DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX` bound by a hair: `deferred_for` = 1810s, 1820s, 1810s, 1930s against a\n1800s bound — overshoots of 10s, 20s, 10s and 130s. Each one killed an in-flight LLM-Review\n(the backfill reconciler retries it, but the gate stalls meanwhile), and they fired at ~10x\nthe normal rate during a 60+-merge landing burst. The bound is sized for a steady-state\nreview queue and is marginally too small for a burst; a bound that misses by 10s buys nothing\nthat a slightly larger bound would not have caught for free.\n\n## Where it lives\n\n- `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:70-82` — the rationale comment + `DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX=\"${DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX:-1800}\"`.\n  Commit 34cd pinned 1800s as **1.5x** `REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (`DEFAULT_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SECONDS\n  = 1200`, `src/rebar/review_bot/config.py:31`).\n- `tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_review_drain.py:399-420` —\n  `test_defer_bound_default_outlasts_the_apps_own_per_review_cap` reads the shell default out of\n  the script and asserts it against the app constant, so the two cannot drift. It currently pins\n  the weaker `bound >= 1x` relationship.\n- `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:133` — an alarm-cadence comment restating `1800s`.\n\n## Decision\n\nRaise the default to **2400s = 2.0x `REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`**, and tighten the pinned test from\n`>= 1x` to `>= 2x` so the new relationship is enforced rather than merely commented.\n\nWhy 2400 and not more: 2400s clears the worst observed overshoot (1930s) by 470s — ~3.6x the\n130s miss — so all four firings would have deferred to completion. Why bounded at all: the\nsaturated-trace analysis on `greasy-plucky-piranha` showed that under *sustained* pipelining an\nunbounded raise does not prevent the kill, it only defers it, while a deploy that never lands\nis its own outage. Anchoring the bound to a fixed multiple of the app's own per-review cap keeps\nit self-adjusting (raising `REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` drags the bound with it, enforced by the\ntest) instead of being a free-floating literal.\n\nNo terraform resource changes — the terraform edit here is comment-only, so nothing in this\nticket requires an operator apply.\n\n## The counter-argument, and why the raise is still bounded\n\n`greasy-plucky-piranha` (7b4a-0f39-1a45-4ce9) closed with an explicit \"I would not raise the\nbound\". Its trace of the 03:03:04Z episode showed `in_flight` oscillating 2 -> 1 -> 2 and\n**never reaching 0 across 30 continuous minutes**: no single review outlived\n`REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, the bound was outlasted by ordinary *pipelining*. Under that\nsaturation a larger bound only defers the same kill. Tonight's sweep (`childsafe-bizarre-lorikeet`\nb902-18af-14ed-4da1, comment 12) presents only the terminal markers, not per-episode `in_flight`\ntraces, so whether the four Aug-12 episodes were saturated the same way is **unestablished**.\n\nThis ticket therefore does not claim to fix saturated episodes. It claims the narrower thing the\ndata does support: four firings missed by 10-130s, and a bound at 2.0x rather than 1.5x the\nper-review cap absorbs those margins. Where an episode really is saturated, 2400s defers the kill\nby 600s and no more — which is precisely why the raise is a fixed multiple of the app's cap and\nnot an unbounded one. The underlying residue piranha carried forward (34cd's rationale treats a\n*concurrent gauge* as if it were a single-review timer) is NOT closed by this ticket and remains\nopen on 7b4a's discovered_from chain.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX` default is 2400 in `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`, with the rationale\n   comment rewritten to state the 2.0x relationship and cite the 2026-08-12 overshoot data.\n2. `test_defer_bound_default_outlasts_the_apps_own_per_review_cap` asserts\n   `bound >= 2 * DEFAULT_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (test updated deliberately, not deleted), with\n   the docstring carrying the same rationale; the test passes.\n3. The `1800s` restatement in `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf` is corrected to 2400s;\n   `terraform fmt -check` and `terraform validate` pass.\n4. Change lands on `main` through Gerrit with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786575422337773001,"uuid":"93434f93-b36b-4987-85a6-3b4dc10e68d4"}